const phoneHref = `tel:+1${phoneRaw}`;
const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/microwave-repair/samsung-not-heating/";

const title = "Samsung Microwave Not Heating? Causes & Fix";
const description = "Samsung microwave runs but doesn't heat? It's the magnetron or high-voltage circuit — and a microwave's HV capacitor can hold a lethal charge even unplugged, so never open it. What's safe to check, repair-vs-replace, when to call. LA, $89.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why does my Samsung microwave run but not heat?",
    a: "When a Samsung microwave lights up, turns the plate, and counts down but the food stays cold, the fault is almost always in the high-voltage circuit that makes the microwaves — the magnetron, the high-voltage diode, the high-voltage capacitor, or the high-voltage transformer — or in a door interlock switch that isn't confirming the door is shut. A failed magnetron is the classic 'runs but won't heat.' None of these are DIY parts: a microwave's high-voltage capacitor can store a dangerous, even lethal, electrical charge for a long time after it's unplugged, so the inside of a microwave is a technician-only area. What you can safely do is rule out non-repair causes (door not sealing, a low power-level setting) and then call. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "Is it safe to open a microwave to fix it myself?",
    a: "No. This is the one appliance where we're unambiguous: do not open the cabinet of a microwave. The high-voltage capacitor inside can hold a lethal electrical charge even when the microwave has been unplugged for a long time, and discharging it wrong can seriously injure or kill you. The magnetron circuit also runs at thousands of volts. Unlike a dishwasher or dryer where you can safely poke around inside once it's unplugged, a microwave is never safe to open at home. Every internal part — magnetron, diode, capacitor, transformer, door switches — is a job for a technician who knows how to safely discharge the capacitor first."
  },
  {
    q: "What can I safely check on a Samsung microwave that won't heat?",
    a: "Only the things that don't require opening it. Confirm the door closes fully and the latch clicks — a microwave won't heat if the door interlock switches don't sense a properly shut door (nothing caught in the seal, no warped door). Check that the power level isn't set low (a 30% power level heats slowly and can feel like 'not heating'), and that you're not in a demo or Sabbath mode. Do a two-minute power-cycle: unplug for a minute and restart. Test with a cup of water so you're judging actual heating, not a cold spot. If it still won't heat after all that, it's the high-voltage circuit — a technician job, not a DIY one."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a Samsung microwave worth repairing if it won't heat?",
    a: "It depends on the type. A countertop Samsung microwave is inexpensive to replace, and a magnetron or high-voltage repair can approach the cost of a new one — so for a basic countertop unit, replacement is often the sensible call, and we'll tell you that honestly. An over-the-range (OTR) or built-in Samsung microwave is a different story: those are expensive, integrated into the cabinetry or venting, and a magnetron or diode repair is usually well worth it versus replacing the whole unit. Our tech diagnoses the exact failed part first, then gives you the real repair-vs-replace numbers so you're not guessing."
  },
  {
    q: "Could it just be the door on my Samsung microwave?",
    a: "It can, and it's worth checking because it's the one no-heat cause that isn't the high-voltage circuit. A microwave has interlock switches that must all confirm the door is fully closed before it will heat — a safety so it never runs with the door open. If the door is slightly warped, the latch is worn, or something's caught in the seal, the switches don't all close and the microwave runs the fan and light and turntable but never powers the magnetron, so it looks like 'runs but won't heat.' Make sure the door shuts cleanly and latches firmly. If it does and there's still no heat, the fault is internal and it's a tech call."
  }
];

