<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Runs but won't heat = the magnetron or high-voltage circuit.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a Samsung microwave lights up, turns the plate, and counts down but the food stays cold, the fault is in the high-voltage circuit that actually makes the microwaves: the magnetron, the high-voltage diode, the high-voltage capacitor, or the high-voltage transformer — or a door interlock switch that isn't confirming the door is shut. A failed magnetron is the classic "runs but won't heat." The one thing you can rule out yourself is the door and your cook settings; everything else is inside, and the inside of a microwave is a technician-only space for a serious safety reason (below). For a countertop Samsung, replacement is often cheaper than the repair; for an over-the-range or built-in, the repair is usually worth it. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout callout-danger"><strong>⚠ Never open a microwave.</strong> A microwave's high-voltage capacitor can hold a lethal electrical charge even after it's been unplugged for a long time. This is the one appliance you never open at home — full safety detail in the next section.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 HV SAFETY (BOLD DIFFERENTIATOR) -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · The one appliance you never open</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A microwave's high-voltage capacitor can kill — even unplugged.</h2>

    <div class="danger-block">
      <p><strong>This is the microwave's version of a gas line: a hazard that overrides any DIY instinct.</strong> Inside every microwave is a high-voltage capacitor that stores electrical energy. Unlike almost every other appliance — where unplugging it makes the inside safe to poke around in — <strong>a microwave capacitor can hold a dangerous, potentially lethal charge for a long time after the unit is unplugged.</strong> The magnetron circuit also operates at thousands of volts.</p>
      <p><strong>So the rule is simple and absolute: never remove the cabinet or reach inside a microwave, plugged in or not.</strong> A trained technician discharges the capacitor safely with the right tool before touching any internal part. There is no homeowner-safe way to test the magnetron, diode, capacitor, or transformer. If the fault is internal, it is a technician job — every time, no exceptions.</p>
    </div>

    <p>That's why this page's "what you can check" list stays entirely on the outside of the microwave — the door, the settings, a power-cycle. If those don't fix it, don't go further; call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Samsung won't heat</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The internal causes — all technician-only.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Magnetron (most common)</h3>
      <p>The tube that generates the microwaves. When it fails, the unit runs normally but produces no heat — the classic "runs but won't heat." Technician replacement after safely discharging the capacitor.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>High-voltage diode</h3>
      <p>Feeds the magnetron. A failed diode stops heating and often takes the capacitor's behavior with it. Technician diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>High-voltage capacitor</h3>
      <p>Stores the charge that drives the magnetron circuit — and the lethal-charge hazard above. A failed capacitor stops heating. Technician-only, discharged first.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>High-voltage transformer</h3>
      <p>Steps voltage up to the thousands the magnetron needs. A failed transformer (sometimes with a burnt smell) stops heat. Technician diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Door interlock switches</h3>
      <p>Several switches must all confirm the door is shut before the magnetron powers. A worn switch or warped door leaves the unit running fan and light but never heating — the one no-heat cause that starts at the (safe) door.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check (outside only)</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Rule these out — then call, don't open it.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Confirm the door shuts fully</strong> and latches firmly, with nothing caught in the seal and no warp — the interlock switches must sense a closed door to heat.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the power level</strong> — a low setting (say 30%) heats slowly and can feel like "not heating." Set it to full power and retest.</li>
      <li><strong>Rule out demo / Sabbath mode</strong> and do a two-minute power-cycle: unplug for a minute, plug back in.</li>
      <li><strong>Test with a cup of water,</strong> not a cold-spot-prone plate, so you're judging real heating.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout callout-danger"><strong>⚠ Where to stop — and this one is absolute:</strong> the door and the settings are the entire safe list. Do not remove the cabinet or reach inside for any reason, plugged in or not. The high-voltage capacitor is a lethal hazard. If the outside checks don't fix it, it's a technician call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 REPAIR VS REPLACE -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Repair or replace?</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Countertop vs. over-the-range changes the answer.</h2>

    <p>Because a magnetron or high-voltage repair carries a real parts-and-labor cost, the type of microwave decides whether it's worth it:</p>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Countertop Samsung — often replace</h3>
      <p>Basic countertop microwaves are inexpensive, so a magnetron or HV repair can approach the price of a new one. We'll tell you honestly when replacing is the smarter money.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Over-the-range or built-in Samsung — usually repair</h3>
      <p>OTR and built-in units are expensive and integrated into the cabinetry and venting, so a magnetron or diode repair is usually well worth it versus replacing the whole unit.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Either way, our tech diagnoses the exact failed part first and gives you the real repair-vs-replace numbers — no guessing, no pressure.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Door and settings fine, still cold? It's the HV circuit.</h2>

    <p>When the door latches cleanly, the power level is full, and the Samsung still won't heat, the fault is the magnetron or high-voltage circuit — technician-only work. Our techs safely discharge the high-voltage capacitor first, then test the magnetron, diode, capacitor, and transformer with a meter and replace the failed part, on OTR, built-in, and countertop units.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/microwave-repair/not-heating/">microwave not heating</a> guide, the same fault on <a href="/services/microwave-repair/lg-not-heating/">LG microwaves</a>, and if the unit is completely dead, <a href="/services/microwave-repair/no-power/">microwave no power</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/microwave-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/microwave-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/microwave-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Samsung microwave not heating: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Samsung microwave won't heat? Leave the HV circuit to us.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

